(Each function is written above the globals it shadows)
To 'shadow above'.
    a text called count is "a text above".
    a list called label is ["a", "list"].
    a number called basket is 7.
    a buffer called total is "a buffer above".
    a map called ratio is {"a": 1}.
    a float called 'the flag' is 2.5.
    Print "above: {count}, {label}, {basket}, {total}, {ratio}, {the flag}".

a number called count is 1.
a text called label is "label".
a buffer called basket is "basket".
a list called total is [1, 2].
a float called ratio is 0.5.
a boolean called 'the flag' is true.

(And each of these is written below them)
To 'shadow below'.
    a text called count is "a text below".
    a list called label is ["b"].
    a number called basket is 8.
    a buffer called total is "a buffer below".
    a map called ratio is {"b": 2}.
    a float called 'the flag' is 3.5.
    Print "below: {count}, {label}, {basket}, {total}, {ratio}, {the flag}".

To 'shadow with a sized buffer and a timer'.
    a buffer called count is 16 bytes in size.
    append "sized" to count.
    a timer called label.
    Start the label.
    Print "sized: {count}, running {the label's running}".

(A function may read the global before its own local of that name)
To 'read the global, then shadow it'.
    Print "the global first: {count}".
    a text called count is "five".
    Print "then the local: {count}".

'shadow above'.
'shadow below'.
'shadow with a sized buffer and a timer'.
'read the global, then shadow it'.
Print "the globals keep their own: {count add 1}, {label}, {basket}, {total}, {ratio}, {the flag}".
